WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.280
Imagine the best school teachers

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:04.800
making millions of dollars a year,

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.940
making more than star baseball players.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.120
That’s already happening one country.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:13.300
It’s something I learned from the PBS series School Inc.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.780
Here’s part 2 of the

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:16.540
Stossel-shortened version

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:19.440
of the documentary made by Andrew Coulson.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.760
Wouldn’t it be great if the best teachers

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.320
earned that same remuneration

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.040
and that same recognition?

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.320
Well here in Korea,

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.360
they do!

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.300
Mr. Choi is a national star.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:35.560
A lot of students have fallen in love with his lectures.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:38.000
In the past, students from outside Seoul had

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.940
to come take these classes during vacation.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.360
But as internet technology improved,

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.780
kids got the opportunity to listen to great lectures

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.740
in the comfort of their own homes.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.140
There aren’t really many celebrity teachers,

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:52.040
so we have to take online courses.

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.140
He was like a celebrity to us and

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:57.220
he actually helped me with the subjects that I did not

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:58.140
really do well on.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.040
He helps a lot of people.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.500
On average

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.400
over 100,000 students taking my lessons.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.340
That’s how he makes millions.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:09.600
And since these most popular teachers make

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.640
the most money

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.300
teachers compete within the market to become,

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:15.000
like,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.100
entertaining and educative at the same time,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:17.740
you know?

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:19.320
Teachers competing to be

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.600
entertaining and educational!

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.820
That happens in the private education market.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.060
Government schools...

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:27.220
they're different.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:33.180
In government schools,

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.580
the best teachers don’t make more money.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.740
Mr Choi is thankful for private schooling.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.680
To be able to share the things that you know

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.780
and get paid for doing that is actually a miracle.

00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:47.720
America’s private school sector is not yet that robust.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.200
But there are now some charter schools

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:51.820
That do make a big difference.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.180
One of Oakland’s worst schools was gradually

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.940
turned into the highest-scoring school in California.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.560
Every racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.520
subgroup of students at these schools

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.560
easily out-performs its statewide peers.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:06.220
And all of them

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.460
beat the average for wealthier white students.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:11.860
The high school has topped the Washington Post's

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:13.520
list of the nation’s best.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.140
Jemila transferred here in 7th grade.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:17.820
This charter was different from her

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.340
government-run school.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:20.700
It was just a

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.480
really big transition because the school that I went to,

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:24.740
they actually gave me

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:26.720
a text book and put us in the back of the classroom

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:28.300
to learn and teach ourselves.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.420
But the enemies of choice keep trying to

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.400
kill off their competition.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.300
They fight charter schools, vouchers,

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:37.700
school tax credits, education savings accounts.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:38.760
They say charter schools

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:39.980
are unaccountable,

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:43.020
because they’re not run by the state.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.740
But real accountability rarely comes from

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.680
government regulators.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.940
Regulators may sleep on the job, or

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:50.920
take bribes, or just

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:52.980
follow meaningless rules.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.500
Real accountability happens

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.500
when parents get to choose

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.280
which school gets their money.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.340
If there were no regulations at all,

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.780
wouldn't some private schools try

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.580
to take advantage of families?

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.480
And wouldn’t the most likely victims be

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:08.100
children whose parents had little schooling themselves

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.260
Here in India,

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:15.360
a lot of schools are taking advantage of poor families.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.200
Back in 1999,

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.580
a team of researchers made surprise visits

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:21.460
to schools around the country.

00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:24.660
In village after village, they found one type of school

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:26.960
that consistently had the worst facilities

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:28.480
and the least learning.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:29.660
Most had leaking roofs,

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:31.400
and barely any had drinking water.

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.580
And if you’re wondering why anyone would

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.160
pay for that that kind of service

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:37.940
it turns out there’s a good reason:

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:39.280
they have to.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:40.900
The findings I just described

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.440
are for public schools.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.660
This teacher talked with a public school teacher.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.320
I said that, “Oh, so what's been going on with you?"

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.420
And she said, “Oh, I managed to get a government job.

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.580
My life is set."

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.160
And I said, “Set in what ways?”

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.140
She said, “Well, I'm never going to be fired.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.560
This is a job for life and it pays me well.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.940
And I don't even have to go.”

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.700
She hasn't been to school

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.640
for the last year-and-a-half since she was

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:10.620
posted there.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.000
Coulson then interviews

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.640
British education professor James Tooley.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.480
Tooley travelled the world to

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:19.840
research how the poorest people educate kids.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:21.740
He went to the slums of India

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:24.560
And I found something which

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.220
which changed my life.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:27.980
I found

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:29.360
a private school,

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.860
a low-cost private school

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:31.780
in those days

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.120
charging the equivalent of about

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.120
one U.S. dollar per month.

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:36.800
I found one,

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.800
and then a second, then a third

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:39.480
and soon,

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.560
I found this federation

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.900
of low-cost private schools

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:46.600
When he told his fellow educators about that,

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.060
they dismissed his discovery.

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.280
“What you’ve found is a couple of businessmen

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.100
ripping off the poor.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:54.640
And anyway, it's only happening

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:55.140
in India.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:55.700
We know about it.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:57.280
"It's a small thing, you know”

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:58.880
And I thought, ripping off the poor? But that's

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.240
not what I have seen there.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:01.860
I’ve seen dedicated

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:04.660
entrepreneurs who are running schools.

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:07.640
They come in on their weekends, and run science fairs,

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.200
you think, “Okay, this must be charity.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.300
maybe the mosques are supporting them,

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:12.480
some NGO,

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.700
some non-government organization is supporting them

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:17.880
When you see 5, 10, 15

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.880
and you hear 500 of these schools

00:05:19.880 --> 00:05:22.280
you realize that can't be the case.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:23.900
There has to be a

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:26.820
financially-sustainable business model.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.260
I talked to a lot of parents and said,

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:31.000
“You're poor," you know

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.900
"Why are you spending money on

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:33.540
the private schools

00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.180
when the government schools are free?”

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:36.100
I’ll never forget, one said,

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.500
"In the government schools,

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:40.860
our children are abandoned."

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:41.680
And sure enough,

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.980
I went to see one of these government schools.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:46.400
130 children sitting on the floor.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:47.080
It's a government school

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:48.540
just around the corner from here,

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:49.820
130 children,

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:51.760
all bright-eyed, eager to learn,

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.900
eager to welcome this stranger in their midst,

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.000
doing nothing,

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:56.960
abandoned.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.500
There are government school regulators.

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:00.820
Tooley interviewed them,

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.140
and they admitted that they take bribes.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:05.180
They were quite up front about it.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:08.220
They came, they sat in the office;

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.140
they sat in a comfortable desk.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:11.300
They took the money;

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.160
they didn't even bother to look

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:14.020
in the school.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:16.100
They had a nice cup of hot chai

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:17.840
and off they went.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:20.560
Dozens of scientific studies have now looked

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:23.120
at these budget private schools in India and

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:24.080
elsewhere.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.160
And what most of them find is that

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:27.020
private schools

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:28.880
are out-performing government schools

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:31.200
that spend three or four times as

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:32.260
much per pupil.

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:34.920
These parents care about their schooling.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.460
They said, “Why are there only 10 computers?

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:39.440
Why was their teacher absent last week?

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:40.480
They care.

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:41.720
They are paying money.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:43.320
And because they're paying, they will

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.160
keep the schools accountable.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:46.480
That's a very important thing

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:48.140
which you mess with at your peril.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.340
That’s just a sample of Andrew Coulson’s

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.320
3 part PBS series,

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:53.480
School Inc.

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:55.180
You can watch the whole thing on the

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:58.200
Free to Choose Network's Youtube page.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.360
Unfortunately,

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.780
Coulson did not live to see his documentary finished.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:05.440
He died while completing it.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:08.680
School Inc is a wonderful memorial to him

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:10.060
and his fine work.

